A||||12||william_thorniii||No Pressure in Hydraulic Clutch System?||||5||
Z||000000||william_thorniii||09-29-2000||01:03 PM||||Friends,<P>Myself and Fiero56 have been scrathing our heads as of late.  I purchased a non-running 86 SE a few months ago thinking that all clutch related problems were due to a bent clutch pedal.  After replacing the pedal another fiero friend (who shall remain nameless... Scott :~p  ) broke the bleeder screw to the slave.  So then the slave was replaced with a wagner product.  The system still wouldn't bleed, so a Wagner Master rebuild kit was ordered... The system still will not bleed... are there any suggestions other than get all new GM parts?  <P>Many Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>85 GT<BR>86 SE<BR>87 EC||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||09-29-2000||01:53 PM||||What exactly do you mean by it won't bleed?||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-29-2000||01:57 PM||||suck all the fluid out of the master and see if anything is blocking the botom of the tank. The hole is small if a flake of leaf or something got in there it could easily block fluid dropping into the MC piston. You may have to take the MC off and turn it upside down to get everything out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||09-29-2000||05:02 PM||||Phil,<P>What is your problem?  Can't you read my mind?  Sorry... I am quite vague sometimes.  When bleeding the system, fluid goes through the MC and comes out the SC just fine... but the pedal does not have any "feel" or in other words, the system does not seem to build up pressure.  Also the engine is not being slowed by the clutch but the car will start with the new pedal.  <P>BTW... I kept trying to get a word with you in Louisville... but never had a good oportunity.  It was the first time having seen your car in person.  Impressive.  <P>Ogre,<P>Thanks for the suggestion... but I think that the MC is fine since I just finished with a MC rebuild.  On this topic though... the reason we determined the MC should be rebuilt was that there was metal particulate lining the MC.  So much so that when watching the SC bleeder screw, we noticed it coming out the back end.  Could this be a clue?<P>Thanks guys... this is my first contribution to the Forum in a LOOOOOOONG time... I appreciate all your help.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-29-2000||08:35 PM||||suspended metal in the fluid usually means a problem. The metal could be rust or junk from the pistons or cyl walls. <P>I think I would have gotten a rebuilt MC with a warranty. I don't rebuild them myself anymore. To much trouble and mess. If you don't hone it just right it ends up leaking anyway. half the time you need to replace the piston.<P>It's note building pressure... Is it loosing fluid?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||09-29-2000||10:14 PM||||One way to check all your seals and stuff is to remove the slave from the tranny (leave fluid line attached) and let the push rod extend. Point the push rod toward the floor and bleed just like the brakes. After you tighten the bleeder screw you shouldn't be able to move the pedal at all, if it moves you have leakage or linkage slop. If all is ok just put the slave back on and you're back in action(perfect bleeding no air)<P>------------------<BR> <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/New-sig.jpg"> <BR>SC 3800||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||09-29-2000||11:24 PM||||If you aren't getting any hydraulic pressure then something is amiss in the hydraulic system (naturally).  That said, the only things that can be wrong are the slave and master cylinders.  <P>You have a leak somewhere in the slave or master cylinder.  I would look at the slave first.  Those aftermarket slaves just don't bleed easily (if ever) and the main problem area is the bleeder screw nipple.  You may need to wrap the bleeder screws threads with some teflon tape to fill in the gap.  <P>I would also look closely at the master......rebuilding the master (or slave) is a hit or miss proposition.  Sometimes it works and sometimes it doesn't.  <P>My advice for you (and I know you don't want to hear it) is to bite the bullet and buy a new genuine GM slave cylinder and/or a new genuine GM master cylinder.<P>The extra expense more than pays for itself the first time you bleed the system....one or two bleeds and it's done.<P>Good luck.....I hope you get it figured out.<BR>    ||||reg||1
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||09-29-2000||11:59 PM||||SUGGESTION:  Come to Boilermaker's Auto Parts and pick up what you need!!!!!!!!!<P>Seriously, I'm not that far away (just ask Scott) and I am there most weekends.  There by appointment also  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierose||09-30-2000||01:12 AM||||I understand what you're going through.  I actually broke the nipple of my bleeder.  Since I was broke as I'm a college student I JB Welded it back on.  It worked for about a month till it stranded me in the middle of nowhere.  But that was before I knew it is possible to drive without the clutch.  Enough rambling<P>85 SE<BR>238,000<P>||||reg||6
Z||000009||william_thorniii||10-02-2000||08:16 AM||||Gentlemen,<P>Thanks for all the help and suggestions.  <P>SE... That use for JB Weld is hilarious.. reminds me of a buddies truck in college.  He even JB Welded the exhaust system.  <P>Phil... that is a good suggestion.  I will try that this week and let you know how it works.  <P>Ogre... I was very afraid of the metallic flakes in the system and hence decided to redo the MC and SC.. I guess we will hope for the best.  <P>Mike V... I knew you would make that suggestion and knew better to buy those garbage ones in the first place.  <P>BM... I might just take you up on that offer if Drew and I can't get it to work out this week.  <P>Later All...<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000010||william_thorniii||10-04-2000||11:46 AM||||BM,<P>Do you have an extra master for me?  I want to try another before I give up on this one and or purchase a new one.  I need the type where the hyd. line comes straight off of the front of the master.  I can send pictures if necessary.  <P>William||||reg||13
Z||000011||862M4inCA||10-04-2000||04:49 PM||||I was just pondering over this question and thought to myself about the line between the MC and SC. I know most of it is metal however, isn't there a rubber line going to the SC from the steel line running under the car? might be worth a look. Even a small pinhole would be enough to introduce air into the system. Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000012||william_thorniii||10-05-2000||08:45 AM||||84,<P>THX for the thought.  I have thoroughly inspected the lines.  I have not found any areas where fluid is escaping which leads me to believe that there are no serious problems with them.  I have heard of instances in which a hole was large enough for air to enter but not allow other fluids to escape... I hope that isn't the case.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
